<p>Denmark will hold a snap parliamentary election on March 24, Prime Minister Mette Frederiksen announced in Copenhagen on Feb. 26. Several outlets linked the decision to rising tensions with the United States and President Trump over Greenland, as well as Frederiksen’s push for a fresh mandate. The Greenland dispute is expected to loom large in the campaign, as Denmark tries to steady its relationship with Washington while managing the status of the autonomous Arctic territory.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/EU4WPfaq8enDoAXPbrDiJ7Vovui1feXMkrPp5NEeAreiwvz9MkHwVou4PVDsGaB8hrLYJ_qmwhHGMo35toCq340_qit-wAOinWpPr773IxeCWAQ-koD1Nw1K18gL99JWeXn8Onl0HfSZLQpBVh25NStnTZuP2t8HVylmpA7I-LdXZSx4L-18yb-wUtpxSC2Jdd4JFciL-5vakLjCHuZn4Bq6-IHnCFRH43OKkqYPAOvR4n-M6uQ64H1oaL7Qp4f79h2JqF6SWN2hgnJzEQ' alt='Danish Prime Minister Mette Frederiksen announces an upcoming parliamentary election in Copenhagen on Feb. 26, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Prime Minister Mette Frederiksen: Frederiksen called a snap election and asked parliament to vote early, after the Greenland standoff with President Trump became a defining political issue. (<a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-denmark-early-election-us-greenland/'>The Globe and Mail</a>)</li><li>Political analysts/experts: Experts quoted by the outlet said Frederiksen seemed to be trying to ride the momentum from standing up to President Trump over Greenland. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/26/world/europe/denmark-election-frederiksen-greenland.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Danish political coverage: Live coverage focused on Frederiksen’s special statement to parliament calling an early election, including her line: ‘It will soon be spring – and the Danes will soon be going to the polls.’ (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2026/feb/26/europe-latest-news-updates-russia-ukraine-war'>The Guardian</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/w